CHANGE ANALYSIS (citations verified, indeterminate)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: depends on state only known at runtime (e.g. feature flags, request data).
The serialization mechanism is clear, but observable deployment impact requires a real request path whose handler returns response.Response; whether such handlers exist or supply metadata is not shown.

HTTP responses returned as response.Response are now preserved through handler processing and serialized as a structured envelope containing data and metadata, while custom headers are still applied. When metadata is absent, the current tested output includes "metadata": null, which conflicts with the updated documentation's claim that the field is omitted.

Likely behavioral changes
  • A handler result whose concrete type is response.Response is now passed to the responder as that type and encoded into an HTTP response envelope containing its Data and Metadata values, rather than exposing only the payload data.
  • For a response.Response with no metadata value, the tested wire representation now contains an explicit metadata:null member alongside data instead of omitting metadata.

Investigate
  • Trace response.Response values from handler return sites through ServeHTTP into Responder.Respond, including whether pointer forms or wrappers can be returned and whether they receive the same metadata and header treatment.
  • Inspect API schemas, compatibility tests, and consumers for assumptions that successful responses contain only data or that absent metadata is omitted rather than encoded as null.
  • Review status-code and error handling when a response.Response and an error are supplied together, since the new branch places both metadata/data and the computed error object into the same internal envelope.

Uncertain
  • The supplied evidence does not identify which registered HTTP routes return response.Response and therefore expose the changed response envelope.
  • It is not established whether every call to Responder.Respond is reached through handler.ServeHTTP or whether other callers can pass response.Response values directly.
  • Whether any real request exercises the new metadata path depends on handlers returning the concrete response.Response value and on the Metadata values they populate.
  • The affected client schemas and consumers, including whether they require metadata to be absent rather than null, are not shown.
  • The surrounding Respond implementation is incomplete here, so the final body and status semantics when response.Response is accompanied by a non-nil error cannot be established.

